-- Data.Time.Zoned.Arithmetic: arithmetic, differences and rounding on
-- an instant in a place (design/datetime.md, layer L3 on L2, lane F).
--
-- The semantics are TC39 Temporal's ZonedDateTime (add, subtract,
-- until, since, round, startOfDay, hoursInDay, with) and its
-- Duration operations with a zoned relativeTo, as rendered by Jiff's
-- `Zoned`. Ported, not redesigned (section 6 of the design note).
--
-- Design law shape.
--   - ADD (AddZonedDateTime). A span with no calendar unit (years,
--     months, weeks and days all zero) is added on the instant, so
--     `zoned-add-span (span-of-hours 1)` is exactly one elapsed hour
--     whatever the clocks did (01:30 -05:00 on New York's spring
--     transition day plus one hour is 03:30 -04:00). Otherwise the
--     years, months, weeks and days are added to the CIVIL date with
--     the day constrained to the target month (2024-05-31 + 1 month is
--     2024-06-30), the time of day is kept, that civil reading is
--     resolved in the zone by the options' `Disambiguation` (T4;
--     `Compatible` by default), and the clock units are then added on
--     the instant. So `+ 1 day` and `+ 24 hours` differ on a
--     transition day, and `zoned-sub-span s` is `zoned-add-span
--     (span-negate s)`.
--   - DIFFERENCE (DifferenceZonedDateTime). With the largest unit
--     `Hour` or smaller (the default is `Hour`) the difference is the
--     exact elapsed time between the instants, and the two values may
--     be in different zones. With `Day` or larger both values must be
--     in the same zone (by name) and the calendar part is counted on
--     the civil readings in the first value's zone: the largest whole
--     count of each unit for which `a` plus that count, read at `a`'s
--     time of day and resolved `Compatible`, does not pass `b`, and
--     the remainder is elapsed time. The remainder is spelled in hours
--     and smaller and is NOT folded into days, so it can reach 24
--     hours and more on a 25-hour day (Temporal spells a zoned
--     difference with TemporalDurationFromInternal(.., hour)).
--     `zoned-since a b` is the negation of `zoned-until a b`, and
--     with no rounding `zoned-add-span (zoned-until a b) a` is `b`
--     for the largest units `Hour` and smaller always, and for `Day`
--     unless `a` is the second occurrence of a repeated civil reading
--     (next point).
--   - A WART THAT IS PORTED. The intermediate reading of a calendar
--     difference is "`a`'s time of day on a date at or next to
--     `b`'s", resolved `Compatible`. When `a` is the SECOND 01:30 of
--     a fold and the calendar part is zero days, that intermediate is
--     the FIRST 01:30, an hour before `a`, so the remainder is an hour
--     longer than the time that elapsed (the second 01:30 on
--     2024-11-03 in New York until 00:30 on the 4th is 24 hours with
--     largest `Day` and 23 by default). This is what Temporal's
--     algorithm answers and it is kept; the default (hours) form is
--     always the elapsed time. Two readings on ONE civil date never
--     go through an intermediate: their difference is the elapsed
--     time.
--   - A DAY is the time between two consecutive civil starts of day in
--     the zone: 23 hours on 2024-03-10 in New York, 25 on 2024-11-03.
--     Rounding a difference or a span uses the same idea from the
--     anchor's time of day: the "day" after the calendar part runs
--     from where that part lands to the same civil time one civil day
--     on. A unit below a day is rounded and compared against that
--     length (NudgeToZonedTime), and rounding to days measures
--     progress through it (NudgeToCalendarUnit): 12 hours 15 minutes
--     from 17:30 on 2024-11-02 rounds DOWN to 0 days, because 17:30 to
--     17:30 the next day is 25 hours.
--   - ROUND a value (ZonedDateTime.prototype.round). To `Day`: within
--     [start of the civil day, start of the next civil day] by the
--     real length of that day. To a smaller unit: the civil reading is
--     rounded (as `datetime-round`) and resolved again preferring the
--     offset the value already has (below).
--   - PREFER THE CURRENT OFFSET (InterpretISODateTimeOffset with the
--     offset option "prefer"). When a civil reading is re-resolved for
--     a value that already has an offset (rounding, and the
--     `zoned-with-` family), the value's own offset is kept if the new
--     reading can have it; otherwise the reading is resolved by the
--     strategy (`Compatible` unless the `-resolving` form names
--     another). So 01:30 -05:00 in the autumn fold stays at -05:00
--     when its minutes are changed.
--   - SPANS RELATIVE TO A ZONED VALUE (`span-round-zoned` and its
--     family) are one construction: add the span to the anchor (ADD,
--     `Compatible`), then take the DIFFERENCE from the anchor to that
--     instant in the units asked for. 2756 hours from 2020-01-01 in
--     Europe/Amsterdam is 3 months 23 days 21 hours (one of those days
--     was 23 hours long; the civil anchor of `Data.Time.Span` gives 20
--     hours).
--   - The civil conveniences (`zoned-tomorrow`, `zoned-first-of-month`,
--     `zoned-nth-weekday`, ..) are lane B's operation on the civil
--     date, the same time of day, resolved `Compatible`.
--
-- Where the engine lives. Lane D's header names a seam (a third
-- `Relative` constructor inside Data.Time.Span). It is not used: that
-- would make Data.Time.Span import the zone modules, and lane D's
-- test harness would have to ship them. The zoned engine is here
-- instead, built on Data.Time.Span's exports (`span-difference-civil`
-- for the calendar count between two dates, `span-difference-nanos`
-- and `span-from-units` for the spelling) and on the rounding kernel
-- of Data.Time.Unit. The one addition to Data.Time.Span is four
-- readers of the abstract `SpanRound` (`span-round-largest`,
-- `-smallest`, `-increment`, `-mode`), so that `span-round-zoned`
-- takes the same options value as `span-round`.
--
-- Overflow. Instants are within +-3.8e20 nanoseconds and a span's
-- clock units total below 1.3e21 (Data.Time.Span's header), so every
-- sum and difference of them formed here is below 2^72 and the
-- `wrapping-` `I128` operations on them are exact. Products with a
-- rounding increment (at most 1e9) and a unit count (at most about
-- 1e9) stay below 2^120; each such product says so where it is
-- formed. `I64` unit counts are span fields (within their limits)
-- plus or minus one increment, so their `wrapping-` sums are exact.
--
-- No operation faults (T2): `Result ZonedError` wherever a refusal is
-- possible, `Option` where the only refusal is the range.
--
-- NAMED REFUSALS:
--   - a silent disambiguation (T4): every operation that resolves a
--     civil reading either takes the strategy (`ZonedArithmetic`, the
--     `-resolving` forms) or says in this header that it is
--     `Compatible` (the conveniences, and the intermediate readings of
--     a difference or a rounding, where Temporal fixes it).
--   - a calendar difference across zones: `ZonedZonesDiffer`. Which
--     zone's days would be counted is not something to pick silently.
--     Move one value with `zoned-with-time-zone` first.
--   - folding the remainder of a calendar difference into days: not
--     done (see DIFFERENCE); a remainder of 24 hours 59 minutes on a
--     25-hour day is spelled so.
--   - `Day` rounding of a value with an increment other than 1, and
--     week, month or year as a value's rounding unit: refused, as in
--     Temporal.
--   - `zoned-hours-in-day` as a number: a day's length is not always a
--     whole number of hours (Lord Howe's half-hour shift), and there
--     is no float in this family. `zoned-day-length` is the exact
--     `Duration`.
--   - operator instances (T2).
--   - rounding at the very end of the range: every rounding of a
--     difference or a span reads the further candidate (the far end of
--     the unit's window, or the civil day after the calendar part) to
--     measure against, and answers `ZonedOutOfRange` when that lies
--     outside the range even if the mode would not have chosen it.
--   - the 0.5.1 reference polyfill as the last word: three steps of
--     the current specification text (read 2026-11-17) are later than
--     it and are followed here, so three kinds of answer differ from
--     that polyfill: two readings on one civil date differ by elapsed
--     time (DifferenceZonedDateTime step 4); a calendar rounding
--     window that does not hold the destination is moved one increment
--     on (ComputeNudgeWindow's additionalShift); and the window's
--     start can be the anchor's own instant (next point).
--   - ONE DELIBERATE DEPARTURE from the specification's letter.
--     ComputeNudgeWindow says "If r1 = 0, let startEpochNs be
--     originEpochNs". Here the window starts at the anchor's own
--     instant only when the whole start candidate is zero (no larger
--     units before it either). For years the two are the same. For a
--     smaller unit after non-zero larger ones (1 month 5 hours rounded
--     to days: r1 is 0 days) the letter would measure the day's
--     progress from the anchor, a month back, and round 5 hours up to
--     a day; that cannot be meant, so the candidate "anchor plus one
--     month" is resolved in the zone as every other candidate is. What
--     the rule is for is kept: an anchor that is the second occurrence
--     of a repeated reading is not resolved again to the first.
module Data.Time.Zoned.Arithmetic where

  import Data.Machine
  import Data.Wide
  import Data.Option
  import Data.Result
  import Data.Ordering
  import Data.String
  import Algebra.Arithmetic
  import Relation.Eq
  import Relation.Ord
  import Data.Time.Unit
  import Data.Time.Duration
  import Data.Time.Timestamp
  import Data.Time.Weekday (Weekday)
  import Data.Time.Date (Date)
  import Data.Time.Date as D
  import Data.Time.TimeOfDay (TimeOfDay)
  import Data.Time.TimeOfDay as T
  import Data.Time.DateTime (DateTime)
  import Data.Time.DateTime as DT
  import Data.Time.Offset
  import Data.Time.Zone
  import Data.Time.Zoned
  import Data.Time.Span
  import Data.Time.Arithmetic

  export ZonedError, ZonedOutOfRange, ZonedRejectedGap, ZonedRejectedFold
  export ZonedZonesDiffer, ZonedBadUnits, ZonedBadIncrement, ZonedUnitLimit
  export ZonedArithmetic, zoned-arithmetic-default, with-disambiguation
  export zoned-arithmetic-disambiguation
  export zoned-add-span, zoned-sub-span, zoned-add-span-with, zoned-sub-span-with
  export zoned-add-duration, zoned-sub-duration
  export zoned-tomorrow, zoned-yesterday
  export zoned-start-of-next-day, zoned-day-length
  export zoned-first-of-month, zoned-last-of-month
  export zoned-first-of-year, zoned-last-of-year
  export zoned-nth-weekday, zoned-nth-weekday-of-month
  export ZonedDifference, zoned-difference-default, zoned-difference-largest
  export zoned-until, zoned-since, zoned-until-with, zoned-since-with
  export ZonedRound, zoned-round-default, zoned-round-to
  export zoned-round, zoned-round-with
  export zoned-with-date, zoned-with-time, zoned-with-datetime
  export zoned-with-date-resolving, zoned-with-time-resolving
  export zoned-with-datetime-resolving
  export span-round-zoned, span-balance-zoned
  export span-total-zoned, span-compare-zoned
  export span-checked-add-zoned, span-checked-sub-zoned


  -- The refusals of this module.
  data ZonedError where
    -- A date, a civil reading or an instant left the supported range.
    ZonedOutOfRange :: ZonedError
    -- `Reject` met a reading that does not exist (offsets before and
    -- after the jump).
    ZonedRejectedGap :: Offset -> Offset -> ZonedError
    -- `Reject` met a reading that occurs twice (the two offsets).
    ZonedRejectedFold :: Offset -> Offset -> ZonedError
    -- A difference in days or larger between values in different
    -- zones.
    ZonedZonesDiffer :: ZonedError
    -- `largest` is smaller than `smallest`, or a unit the operation
    -- does not accept.
    ZonedBadUnits :: ZonedError
    -- The rounding increment is not valid for the smallest unit.
    ZonedBadIncrement :: ZonedError
    -- A unit of the resulting span passes its limit.
    ZonedUnitLimit :: ZonedError

  -- -----------------------------------------------------------------
  -- Private plumbing. The header gives the bounds that keep each
  -- wrapping use from wrapping.
  -- -----------------------------------------------------------------

  plus :: I64 -> I64 -> I64
  plus a b = wrapping-add-i64 a b

  minus :: I64 -> I64 -> I64
  minus a b = wrapping-sub-i64 a b

  times :: I64 -> I64 -> I64
  times a b = wrapping-mul-i64 a b

  -- Truncated quotient by a divisor of at least 1. The guard excludes
  -- the divisors for which the checked operation answers `None`.
  quot :: I64 -> I64 -> I64
  quot a d =
    if d < 1 then 0
    else case checked-div-i64 a d of
      Some q -> q
      None -> 0

  sgn :: I64 -> I64
  sgn n = if n > 0 then 1 else if n < 0 then -1 else 0

  -- A typed two-way choice (a literal `if` in a `let` defaults to
  -- `Int`).
  choose :: Bool -> I64 -> I64 -> I64
  choose c a b = if c then a else b

  wide :: I64 -> I128
  wide n = i64-to-i128 n

  add128 :: I128 -> I128 -> I128
  add128 a b = wrapping-add-i128 a b

  sub128 :: I128 -> I128 -> I128
  sub128 a b = wrapping-sub-i128 a b

  mul128 :: I128 -> I128 -> I128
  mul128 a b = wrapping-mul-i128 a b

  sgn128 :: I128 -> I64
  sgn128 n = if gt-i128 n 0 then 1 else if lt-i128 n 0 then -1 else 0

  -- The magnitude of a value bounded far inside `I128`.
  abs128 :: I128 -> I128
  abs128 n = if lt-i128 n 0 then wrapping-neg-i128 n else n

  nanos :: Timestamp -> I128
  nanos t = timestamp-to-unix-nanos t

  of-span-error :: SpanError -> ZonedError
  of-span-error e = case e of
    SpanSignConflict -> ZonedUnitLimit
    SpanUnitLimit -> ZonedUnitLimit
    SpanNeedsAnchor -> ZonedBadUnits
    SpanBadUnits -> ZonedBadUnits
    SpanBadIncrement -> ZonedBadIncrement
    SpanOutOfRange -> ZonedOutOfRange

  of-ambiguity :: AmbiguityError -> ZonedError
  of-ambiguity e = case e of
    RejectedGap b a -> ZonedRejectedGap b a
    RejectedFold b a -> ZonedRejectedFold b a
    AmbiguityOutOfRange -> ZonedOutOfRange

  -- The instant of a civil date and time of day in the zone, by the
  -- strategy.
  resolve :: Disambiguation -> TimeZone -> Date -> TimeOfDay -> Result ZonedError Timestamp
  resolve s z d t = case zone-to-timestamp s z (DT.datetime d t) of
    Err e -> Err (of-ambiguity e)
    Ok ts -> Ok ts

  -- The same, `Compatible`, as a nanosecond count (Temporal's
  -- GetEpochNanosecondsFor(.., compatible)).
  epoch :: TimeZone -> Date -> TimeOfDay -> Result ZonedError I128
  epoch z d t = case resolve Compatible z d t of
    Err e -> Err e
    Ok ts -> Ok (nanos ts)

  -- An instant moved by a nanosecond count (AddInstant).
  instant-plus :: Timestamp -> I128 -> Result ZonedError Timestamp
  instant-plus ts n = case timestamp-from-unix-nanos-i128 (add128 (nanos ts) n) of
    None -> Err ZonedOutOfRange
    Some r -> Ok r

  same-zone :: Zoned -> Zoned -> Bool
  same-zone a b = zone-name (zoned-time-zone a) == zone-name (zoned-time-zone b)

  in-zone :: TimeZone -> Result ZonedError Timestamp -> Result ZonedError Zoned
  in-zone z r = case r of
    Err e -> Err e
    Ok ts -> Ok (zoned-from-timestamp z ts)

  -- -----------------------------------------------------------------
  -- The internal duration (Temporal's Internal Duration Record): the
  -- four date units, signed, and the clock units as one nanosecond
  -- count.
  -- -----------------------------------------------------------------

  data Parts where
    MkParts :: I64 -> I64 -> I64 -> I64 -> I128 -> Parts

  zero-parts :: Parts
  zero-parts = MkParts 0 0 0 0 0

  date-only :: I64 -> I64 -> I64 -> I64 -> Parts
  date-only y mo w d = MkParts y mo w d 0

  parts-of :: forall (c :: SpanClass). Span c -> Parts
  parts-of s = MkParts (span-years s) (span-months s) (span-weeks s) (span-days s) (span-time-nanos s)

  -- Whether the date part is zero (DateDurationSign = 0).
  no-date :: Parts -> Bool
  no-date p = case p of
    MkParts y mo w d _ -> y == 0 && mo == 0 && w == 0 && d == 0

  -- InternalDurationSign as Temporal's rounding "sign": -1 when
  -- negative, otherwise 1.
  direction :: Parts -> I64
  direction p = case p of
    MkParts y mo w d t ->
      if y /= 0 then choose (y < 0) (-1) 1
      else if mo /= 0 then choose (mo < 0) (-1) 1
      else if w /= 0 then choose (w < 0) (-1) 1
      else if d /= 0 then choose (d < 0) (-1) 1
      else choose (lt-i128 t 0) (-1) 1

  -- CalendarDateAdd with "constrain" (Data.Time.Span's `add-date`):
  -- years and months as one month count, the day constrained once,
  -- then weeks and days on the epoch day. Lane B's operations check
  -- the range.
  add-date :: I64 -> I64 -> I64 -> I64 -> Date -> Option Date
  add-date y mo w d x =
    let months = plus (times y 12) mo in
    let days = plus (times w 7) d in
    if months == 0 then (if days == 0 then Some x else D.add-days days x)
    else case D.add-months-constrain months x of
      None -> None
      Some m -> if days == 0 then Some m else D.add-days days m

  add-date-parts :: Parts -> Date -> Result ZonedError Date
  add-date-parts p x = case p of
    MkParts y mo w d _ -> case add-date y mo w d x of
      None -> Err ZonedOutOfRange
      Some r -> Ok r

  -- TemporalDurationFromInternal(.., hour) for a zoned result: the
  -- clock nanoseconds are spelled in hours and smaller and are not
  -- folded into the days (header, DIFFERENCE).
  spell :: Parts -> Result ZonedError (Span Calendar)
  spell p = case p of
    MkParts y mo w d t -> case span-difference-nanos Hour Nanosecond 1 Trunc t of
      Err e -> Err (of-span-error e)
      Ok c -> case span-from-units y mo w d (span-hours c) (span-minutes c) (span-seconds c) (span-milliseconds c) (span-microseconds c) (span-nanoseconds c) of
        Err e -> Err (of-span-error e)
        Ok s -> Ok s

  -- -----------------------------------------------------------------
  -- Addition (AddZonedDateTime).
  -- -----------------------------------------------------------------

  add-core :: Disambiguation -> TimeZone -> Timestamp -> Parts -> Result ZonedError Timestamp
  add-core s z ts p = case p of
    MkParts _ _ _ _ t ->
      if no-date p then instant-plus ts t
      else
        let civil = zone-to-datetime z ts in
        case add-date-parts p (DT.date-part civil) of
          Err e -> Err e
          Ok nd -> case resolve s z nd (DT.time-part civil) of
            Err e -> Err e
            Ok mid -> instant-plus mid t

  -- The options of zoned addition (T7): how a civil reading that the
  -- calendar units land on is resolved when it does not exist or
  -- occurs twice.
  data ZonedArithmetic where
    MkZonedArithmetic :: Disambiguation -> ZonedArithmetic

  -- `Compatible`.
  zoned-arithmetic-default :: ZonedArithmetic
  zoned-arithmetic-default = MkZonedArithmetic Compatible

  with-disambiguation :: Disambiguation -> ZonedArithmetic -> ZonedArithmetic
  with-disambiguation s o = MkZonedArithmetic s

  zoned-arithmetic-disambiguation :: ZonedArithmetic -> Disambiguation
  zoned-arithmetic-disambiguation o = case o of
    MkZonedArithmetic s -> s

  -- Add a span (the header's ADD law) under the options.
  --   Err ZonedOutOfRange    the date or the instant leaves the range
  --   Err ZonedRejectedGap,
  --       ZonedRejectedFold  the options say `Reject` and the calendar
  --                          units land on such a reading
  zoned-add-span-with :: forall (c :: SpanClass). ZonedArithmetic -> Span c -> Zoned -> Result ZonedError Zoned
  zoned-add-span-with o s x = case o of
    MkZonedArithmetic dis ->
      let z = zoned-time-zone x in
      in-zone z (add-core dis z (zoned-timestamp x) (parts-of s))

  zoned-sub-span-with :: forall (c :: SpanClass). ZonedArithmetic -> Span c -> Zoned -> Result ZonedError Zoned
  zoned-sub-span-with o s x = zoned-add-span-with o (span-negate s) x

  -- The same, `Compatible`: 2024-05-31T13:45 in New York plus one
  -- month is 2024-06-30T13:45 there.
  zoned-add-span :: forall (c :: SpanClass). Span c -> Zoned -> Result ZonedError Zoned
  zoned-add-span s x = zoned-add-span-with zoned-arithmetic-default s x

  zoned-sub-span :: forall (c :: SpanClass). Span c -> Zoned -> Result ZonedError Zoned
  zoned-sub-span s x = zoned-add-span-with zoned-arithmetic-default (span-negate s) x

  -- Exact elapsed time, on the instant. `None` when the instant leaves
  -- the range.
  zoned-add-duration :: Duration -> Zoned -> Option Zoned
  zoned-add-duration d x = case timestamp-checked-add (zoned-timestamp x) d of
    None -> None
    Some t -> Some (zoned-from-timestamp (zoned-time-zone x) t)

  zoned-sub-duration :: Duration -> Zoned -> Option Zoned
  zoned-sub-duration d x = case timestamp-checked-sub (zoned-timestamp x) d of
    None -> None
    Some t -> Some (zoned-from-timestamp (zoned-time-zone x) t)

  -- -----------------------------------------------------------------
  -- Days: the start of a civil day, the next day, a day's length, and
  -- the civil conveniences.
  -- -----------------------------------------------------------------

  -- The first instant of a civil date in the zone (Temporal's
  -- GetStartOfDay; the same rule as `zoned-start-of-day`, for a date
  -- rather than a value): midnight when it exists, its first
  -- occurrence when it occurs twice, and when it was skipped the
  -- transition that skipped it, which is the next transition after
  -- midnight read with the offset that follows the gap.
  start-of-date :: TimeZone -> Date -> Option Timestamp
  start-of-date z d =
    let midnight = DT.from-date d in
    case zone-to-ambiguous z midnight of
      Unambiguous o -> offset-datetime-to-timestamp o midnight
      Fold before _ -> offset-datetime-to-timestamp before midnight
      Gap before after -> case offset-datetime-to-timestamp after midnight of
        None -> offset-datetime-to-timestamp before midnight
        Some early -> case zone-next-transition-unix-seconds z (timestamp-to-unix-seconds early) of
          None -> offset-datetime-to-timestamp before midnight
          Some s -> timestamp-from-unix-seconds s

  -- The first instant of the civil day after `x`'s. `None` at the end
  -- of the range. (When a zone skipped a whole civil day, as
  -- Pacific/Apia skipped 2011-12-30, this is the start of the day
  -- after the skipped one, as in Temporal.)
  zoned-start-of-next-day :: Zoned -> Option Zoned
  zoned-start-of-next-day x = case D.tomorrow (zoned-date x) of
    None -> None
    Some d -> case start-of-date (zoned-time-zone x) d of
      None -> None
      Some t -> Some (zoned-from-timestamp (zoned-time-zone x) t)

  -- The length of `x`'s civil day in its zone (Temporal's hoursInDay,
  -- exact): from the start of the day to the start of the next.
  -- 23 hours on 2024-03-10 in New York, 25 on 2024-11-03. `None` at
  -- the two ends of the range.
  zoned-day-length :: Zoned -> Option Duration
  zoned-day-length x = case start-of-date (zoned-time-zone x) (zoned-date x) of
    None -> None
    Some a -> case zoned-start-of-next-day x of
      None -> None
      Some b -> Some (timestamp-until a (zoned-timestamp b))

  -- The same time of day on another civil date, `Compatible`.
  on-date :: Zoned -> Option Date -> Result ZonedError Zoned
  on-date x od = case od of
    None -> Err ZonedOutOfRange
    Some d ->
      let z = zoned-time-zone x in
      in-zone z (resolve Compatible z d (zoned-time-of-day x))

  -- The same civil time on the next civil day, `Compatible`: tomorrow
  -- of 2024-11-02T17:25 in New York is 2024-11-03T17:25, 25 hours
  -- later; of 2024-03-09T02:30 it is 2024-03-10T03:30 (02:30 does not
  -- exist). For another strategy add `span-of-days 1` with options.
  --   Err ZonedOutOfRange  the end of the range
  zoned-tomorrow :: Zoned -> Result ZonedError Zoned
  zoned-tomorrow x = on-date x (D.tomorrow (zoned-date x))

  zoned-yesterday :: Zoned -> Result ZonedError Zoned
  zoned-yesterday x = on-date x (D.yesterday (zoned-date x))

  -- Lane B's civil operation on the date, the time of day kept,
  -- resolved `Compatible`.
  zoned-first-of-month :: Zoned -> Result ZonedError Zoned
  zoned-first-of-month x = on-date x (Some (D.first-of-month (zoned-date x)))

  zoned-last-of-month :: Zoned -> Result ZonedError Zoned
  zoned-last-of-month x = on-date x (Some (D.last-of-month (zoned-date x)))

  zoned-first-of-year :: Zoned -> Result ZonedError Zoned
  zoned-first-of-year x = on-date x (Some (D.first-of-year (zoned-date x)))

  zoned-last-of-year :: Zoned -> Result ZonedError Zoned
  zoned-last-of-year x = on-date x (Some (D.last-of-year (zoned-date x)))

  -- `Err ZonedOutOfRange` also when the civil operation refuses (`n`
  -- of zero, a weekday the month does not have that many of).
  zoned-nth-weekday :: I64 -> Weekday -> Zoned -> Result ZonedError Zoned
  zoned-nth-weekday n wd x = on-date x (D.nth-weekday n wd (zoned-date x))

  zoned-nth-weekday-of-month :: I64 -> Weekday -> Zoned -> Result ZonedError Zoned
  zoned-nth-weekday-of-month n wd x = on-date x (D.nth-weekday-of-month n wd (zoned-date x))

  -- -----------------------------------------------------------------
  -- Difference (DifferenceZonedDateTime), largest unit `Day` or
  -- larger.
  -- -----------------------------------------------------------------

  -- From instant `a` to instant `b` in zone `z`. The calendar part is
  -- counted between `a`'s civil date and an intermediate date at or
  -- next to `b`'s, chosen so that the intermediate reading (that date
  -- at `a`'s time of day, `Compatible`) does not pass `b`; the rest is
  -- elapsed time. Two readings on one civil date differ by elapsed
  -- time only.
  --
  -- `back` is the specification's "sign": 1 when `b` is before `a`,
  -- otherwise -1. The intermediate starts at `b`'s date, one day
  -- nearer `a` when the times of day already show it would pass `b`,
  -- and may move one day more, or two going forward (a reading pushed
  -- forward out of a gap can pass `b` a second time: noon on
  -- 2011-12-28 until 06:00 on the 31st in Pacific/Apia).
  diff-zoned :: TimeZone -> Timestamp -> Timestamp -> TimeUnit -> Result ZonedError Parts
  diff-zoned z a b largest =
    let n1 = nanos a in
    let n2 = nanos b in
    if eq-i128 n1 n2 then Ok zero-parts
    else
      let s = zone-to-datetime z a in
      let e = zone-to-datetime z b in
      if DT.date-part s == DT.date-part e then Ok (MkParts 0 0 0 0 (sub128 n2 n1))
      else
        let back = choose (lt-i128 n2 n1) 1 (-1) in
        let limit = choose (back == 1) 1 2 in
        let lead = sgn (T.diff-nanos (DT.time-part s) (DT.time-part e)) in
        let first = choose (lead == back) 1 0 in
        correct z s (DT.date-part e) n2 back first limit largest

  -- The day-correction loop. Running past `limit` cannot happen (the
  -- specification asserts it); it reads as out of range.
  correct :: TimeZone -> DateTime -> Date -> I128 -> I64 -> I64 -> I64 -> TimeUnit -> Result ZonedError Parts
  correct z s edate n2 back c limit largest =
    if c > limit then Err ZonedOutOfRange
    else case D.add-days (times c back) edate of
      None -> Err ZonedOutOfRange
      Some mid -> case epoch z mid (DT.time-part s) of
        Err e -> Err e
        Ok m ->
          let t = sub128 n2 m in
          if sgn128 t /= back then between (DT.date-part s) mid t largest
          else correct z s edate n2 back (plus c 1) limit largest

  -- CalendarDateUntil between the two dates (lane D's engine on two
  -- midnights, exact), with the elapsed time attached.
  between :: Date -> Date -> I128 -> TimeUnit -> Result ZonedError Parts
  between sdate mid t largest =
    case span-difference-civil (unit-larger-of largest Day) Nanosecond 1 Trunc (DT.from-date sdate) (DT.from-date mid) of
      Err e -> Err (of-span-error e)
      Ok sp -> Ok (MkParts (span-years sp) (span-months sp) (span-weeks sp) (span-days sp) t)

  -- -----------------------------------------------------------------
  -- Rounding relative to a zoned anchor (RoundRelativeDuration with a
  -- time zone).
  -- -----------------------------------------------------------------

  -- The two candidates around a duration for a calendar unit: the
  -- unit's value truncated to the increment (`r1`), the two date
  -- durations (that and one increment further from zero), and the
  -- instants they land on from the anchor.
  data Window where
    MkWindow :: I64 -> Parts -> Parts -> I128 -> I128 -> Window

  trunc-to :: I64 -> I64 -> I64
  trunc-to inc v = times (quot v inc) inc

  -- The instant of the anchor's civil reading moved by a date
  -- duration; the anchor's own instant when the duration is zero
  -- (header, last refusal).
  landing :: TimeZone -> I128 -> DateTime -> Parts -> Result ZonedError I128
  landing z origin-ns origin p =
    if no-date p then Ok origin-ns
    else case add-date-parts p (DT.date-part origin) of
      Err e -> Err e
      Ok d -> epoch z d (DT.time-part origin)

  window-at :: TimeZone -> I128 -> DateTime -> I64 -> Parts -> Parts -> Result ZonedError Window
  window-at z origin-ns origin r1 sp ep = case landing z origin-ns origin sp of
    Err e -> Err e
    Ok sn -> case landing z origin-ns origin ep of
      Err e -> Err e
      Ok en ->
        if eq-i128 sn en then Err ZonedOutOfRange
        else Ok (MkWindow r1 sp ep sn en)

  -- ComputeNudgeWindow. `shift` (the specification's additionalShift)
  -- moves a year or month window one increment further; weeks and days
  -- ignore it. (For a week: a week is seven days on every date, so the
  -- whole weeks in the duration's days are the days divided by seven,
  -- truncated.)
  window :: TimeZone -> I128 -> DateTime -> I64 -> Parts -> I64 -> TimeUnit -> Bool -> Result ZonedError Window
  window z origin-ns origin sign p inc unit shift = case p of
    MkParts y mo w d _ ->
      let step = times inc sign in
      let extra = choose shift step 0 in
      case unit of
        Year ->
          let r1 = plus (trunc-to inc y) extra in
          window-at z origin-ns origin r1 (date-only r1 0 0 0) (date-only (plus r1 step) 0 0 0)
        Month ->
          let r1 = plus (trunc-to inc mo) extra in
          window-at z origin-ns origin r1 (date-only y r1 0 0) (date-only y (plus r1 step) 0 0)
        Week ->
          let r1 = trunc-to inc (plus w (quot d 7)) in
          window-at z origin-ns origin r1 (date-only y mo r1 0) (date-only y mo (plus r1 step) 0)
        Day ->
          let r1 = trunc-to inc d in
          window-at z origin-ns origin r1 (date-only y mo w r1) (date-only y mo w (plus r1 step))
        Hour -> Err ZonedBadUnits
        Minute -> Err ZonedBadUnits
        Second -> Err ZonedBadUnits
        Millisecond -> Err ZonedBadUnits
        Microsecond -> Err ZonedBadUnits
        Nanosecond -> Err ZonedBadUnits

  -- Whether the destination lies in the window (either end included),
  -- in the direction of travel.
  inside :: I128 -> Window -> Bool
  inside dest win = case win of
    MkWindow _ _ _ sn en ->
      (le-i128 sn dest && le-i128 dest en) || (le-i128 en dest && le-i128 dest sn)

  -- The window the destination is measured in, and whether it had to
  -- be shifted (the first steps of NudgeToCalendarUnit). The
  -- destination can lie beyond the first window: 2024-01-31T12:00
  -- until 2025-03-01T11:00 is 1 year 28 days 23 hours, yet the month
  -- window after the year ends at 2025-02-28T12:00 (the day
  -- constrained), before the destination; the window one month on
  -- holds it.
  settled :: TimeZone -> I128 -> I128 -> DateTime -> I64 -> Parts -> I64 -> TimeUnit -> Result ZonedError (Window, Bool)
  settled z origin-ns dest origin sign p inc unit = case window z origin-ns origin sign p inc unit False of
    Err e -> Err e
    Ok win ->
      if inside dest win then Ok (win, False)
      else case window z origin-ns origin sign p inc unit True of
        Err e -> Err e
        Ok again -> Ok (again, True)

  -- What a nudge produced: the rounded duration, the instant it lands
  -- on, and whether it moved to the further candidate.
  data Nudge where
    MkNudge :: Parts -> I128 -> Bool -> Nudge

  -- Whether a calendar nudge moves to the further candidate
  -- (Temporal's GetUnsignedRoundingMode and ApplyUnsignedRoundingMode
  -- on the progress `num / den` through the window, both magnitudes;
  -- progress of exactly 1 is the further candidate under every mode).
  expands :: RoundMode -> Bool -> I128 -> I128 -> Bool -> Bool
  expands mode negative num den even =
    if eq-i128 num 0 then False
    else if eq-i128 num den then True
    else
      let twice = add128 num num in
      let over = gt-i128 twice den in
      let tie = eq-i128 twice den in
      case mode of
        Trunc -> False
        Expand -> True
        Ceil -> not negative
        Floor -> negative
        HalfExpand -> over || tie
        HalfTrunc -> over
        HalfCeil -> over || (tie && not negative)
        HalfFloor -> over || (tie && negative)
        HalfEven -> over || (tie && not even)

  -- Whether `r1 / inc` is even (`inc` is at least 1 and divides `r1`).
  even-count :: I64 -> I64 -> Bool
  even-count r1 inc = case checked-rem-i64 (quot r1 inc) 2 of
    None -> True
    Some r -> r == 0

  -- NudgeToCalendarUnit: `den` is the window's length and `num` the
  -- distance from its start to the destination (both below 7.6e20, so
  -- doubling `num` is exact). A shifted window counts as an expansion
  -- whichever candidate is chosen. The time of the duration is
  -- dropped: the result is a whole number of the unit.
  nudge-calendar :: TimeZone -> I128 -> I128 -> DateTime -> I64 -> Parts -> I64 -> TimeUnit -> RoundMode -> Result ZonedError Nudge
  nudge-calendar z origin-ns dest origin sign p inc unit mode = case settled z origin-ns dest origin sign p inc unit of
    Err e -> Err e
    Ok pair -> case pair of
      (win, shifted) -> case win of
        MkWindow r1 sp ep sn en ->
          let den = abs128 (sub128 en sn) in
          let num = abs128 (sub128 dest sn) in
          if expands mode (sign < 0) num den (even-count r1 inc) then Ok (MkNudge ep en True)
          else Ok (MkNudge sp sn shifted)

  -- NudgeToZonedTime, for a smallest unit of `Hour` or smaller: the
  -- time is rounded on its own; when the rounded time reaches the
  -- length of the day that follows the date part (from the date part's
  -- landing to one civil day further, both at the anchor's time of
  -- day), that day joins the date part and the time beyond it is
  -- rounded again.
  --
  -- Overflow: the quantum is at most 3600e9 * 1e9 < 2^72 and the time
  -- below 2^72, so the kernel cannot refuse; its `None` arm is mapped
  -- to a refusal anyway.
  nudge-zoned-time :: TimeZone -> DateTime -> I64 -> Parts -> I64 -> TimeUnit -> RoundMode -> Result ZonedError Nudge
  nudge-zoned-time z origin sign p inc unit mode = case p of
    MkParts y mo w d t -> case unit-nanos unit of
      None -> Err ZonedBadUnits
      Some len -> case add-date y mo w d (DT.date-part origin) of
        None -> Err ZonedOutOfRange
        Some start -> case D.add-days sign start of
          None -> Err ZonedOutOfRange
          Some after -> case epoch z start (DT.time-part origin) of
            Err e -> Err e
            Ok sn -> case epoch z after (DT.time-part origin) of
              Err e -> Err e
              Ok en -> nudge-in-day sign p (mul128 len (wide inc)) mode sn en

  nudge-in-day :: I64 -> Parts -> I128 -> RoundMode -> I128 -> I128 -> Result ZonedError Nudge
  nudge-in-day sign p quantum mode sn en = case p of
    MkParts y mo w d t -> case round-i128 mode quantum t of
      None -> Err ZonedUnitLimit
      Some rounded ->
        let beyond = sub128 rounded (sub128 en sn) in
        if sgn128 beyond /= minus 0 sign then
          case round-i128 mode quantum beyond of
            None -> Err ZonedUnitLimit
            Some again -> Ok (MkNudge (MkParts y mo w (plus d sign) again) (add128 en again) True)
        else Ok (MkNudge (MkParts y mo w d rounded) (add128 sn rounded) False)

  -- The duration one step further in the next larger unit, everything
  -- below it cleared (BubbleRelativeDuration's endDuration).
  bubble-end :: I64 -> Parts -> TimeUnit -> Parts
  bubble-end sign p u = case p of
    MkParts y mo w _ _ ->
      if unit-eq u Year then date-only (plus y sign) 0 0 0
      else if unit-eq u Month then date-only y (plus mo sign) 0 0
      else date-only y mo (plus w sign) 0

  -- BubbleRelativeDuration: after a nudge that expanded, carry into
  -- each larger unit up to `largest` while the nudged instant reaches
  -- it. Weeks are tried only when `largest` is `Week`. `unit` is the
  -- unit already settled.
  bubble :: TimeZone -> I128 -> DateTime -> I64 -> Parts -> I128 -> TimeUnit -> TimeUnit -> Result ZonedError Parts
  bubble z origin-ns origin sign p nudged largest unit =
    if unit-rank unit >= unit-rank largest then Ok p
    else case unit-next-larger unit of
      None -> Ok p
      Some u ->
        if unit-eq u Week && not (unit-eq largest Week) then bubble z origin-ns origin sign p nudged largest u
        else
          let ep = bubble-end sign p u in
          case landing z origin-ns origin ep of
            Err e -> Err e
            Ok en ->
              if sgn128 (sub128 nudged en) /= minus 0 sign then bubble z origin-ns origin sign ep nudged largest u
              else Ok p

  -- RoundRelativeDuration with a zone: day and larger through the
  -- calendar nudge (a zoned day has no fixed length), smaller units
  -- through the zoned-time nudge; an expansion is carried upward,
  -- except from weeks.
  round-relative :: TimeZone -> Timestamp -> Timestamp -> Parts -> TimeUnit -> I64 -> TimeUnit -> RoundMode -> Result ZonedError Parts
  round-relative z a b p largest inc smallest mode =
    let sign = direction p in
    let origin = zone-to-datetime z a in
    let origin-ns = nanos a in
    let n = if unit-is-calendar smallest then nudge-calendar z origin-ns (nanos b) origin sign p inc smallest mode
            else nudge-zoned-time z origin sign p inc smallest mode in
    case n of
      Err e -> Err e
      Ok r -> case r of
        MkNudge q nudged expanded ->
          if expanded && not (unit-eq smallest Week) then bubble z origin-ns origin sign q nudged largest (unit-larger-of smallest Day)
          else Ok q

  -- DifferenceZonedDateTimeWithRounding and the spelling. The caller
  -- has checked the order of the units; the increment is checked
  -- here.
  diff-rounded :: TimeZone -> Timestamp -> Timestamp -> TimeUnit -> TimeUnit -> I64 -> RoundMode -> Result ZonedError (Span Calendar)
  diff-rounded z a b largest smallest inc mode =
    if not (unit-is-calendar largest) then
      case span-difference-nanos largest smallest inc mode (sub128 (nanos b) (nanos a)) of
        Err e -> Err (of-span-error e)
        Ok s -> Ok (span-to-calendar s)
    else if unit-rank largest < unit-rank smallest then Err ZonedBadUnits
    else if not (unit-span-increment-ok smallest inc) then Err ZonedBadIncrement
    else case diff-zoned z a b largest of
      Err e -> Err e
      Ok p ->
        if unit-eq smallest Nanosecond && inc == 1 then spell p
        else case round-relative z a b p largest inc smallest mode of
          Err e -> Err e
          Ok q -> spell q

  -- -----------------------------------------------------------------
  -- until and since.
  -- -----------------------------------------------------------------

  -- The options of a zoned difference (T7): largest unit (unset: the
  -- larger of `Hour` and the smallest unit), smallest unit (unset:
  -- `Nanosecond`), increment, mode.
  data ZonedDifference where
    MkZonedDifference :: Option TimeUnit -> Option TimeUnit -> I64 -> RoundMode -> ZonedDifference

  -- Both units unset, increment 1, `Trunc`.
  zoned-difference-default :: ZonedDifference
  zoned-difference-default = MkZonedDifference None None 1 Trunc

  -- The common case: the default with a largest unit.
  zoned-difference-largest :: TimeUnit -> ZonedDifference
  zoned-difference-largest u = MkZonedDifference (Some u) None 1 Trunc

  implicit instance LargestOption_ZonedDifference :: LargestOption ZonedDifference where
    with-largest u o = case o of
      MkZonedDifference _ s i m -> MkZonedDifference (Some u) s i m

  implicit instance RoundingOptions_ZonedDifference :: RoundingOptions ZonedDifference where
    with-smallest u o = case o of
      MkZonedDifference l _ i m -> MkZonedDifference l (Some u) i m
    with-increment n o = case o of
      MkZonedDifference l s _ m -> MkZonedDifference l s n m
    with-mode md o = case o of
      MkZonedDifference l s i _ -> MkZonedDifference l s i md

  negated :: Result ZonedError (Span Calendar) -> Result ZonedError (Span Calendar)
  negated r = case r of
    Err e -> Err e
    Ok s -> Ok (span-negate s)

  -- GetDifferenceSettings and DifferenceTemporalZonedDateTime, from
  -- `a` to `b`. `negate` is set for `since` (the mode is negated here
  -- and the result by the caller).
  difference :: Bool -> ZonedDifference -> Zoned -> Zoned -> Result ZonedError (Span Calendar)
  difference negate o a b = case o of
    MkZonedDifference l s inc mode ->
      let smallest = case s of
                       Some u -> u
                       None -> Nanosecond in
      let largest = case l of
                      Some u -> u
                      None -> unit-larger-of Hour smallest in
      if unit-rank largest < unit-rank smallest then Err ZonedBadUnits
      else if not (unit-span-increment-ok smallest inc) then Err ZonedBadIncrement
      else if unit-is-calendar largest && not (same-zone a b) then Err ZonedZonesDiffer
      else diff-rounded (zoned-time-zone a) (zoned-timestamp a) (zoned-timestamp b) largest smallest inc (if negate then round-mode-negate mode else mode)

  -- The elapsed time from `a` to `b` in hours and smaller, exact; the
  -- zones may differ. 2024-11-02T17:25 until 2024-11-03T17:25 in New
  -- York is 25 hours. Total: the whole timestamp range is inside the
  -- hour limit, so the `Err` arm is unreachable.
  zoned-until :: Zoned -> Zoned -> Span Clock
  zoned-until a b =
    case span-difference-nanos Hour Nanosecond 1 Trunc (sub128 (nanos (zoned-timestamp b)) (nanos (zoned-timestamp a))) of
      Ok s -> s
      Err _ -> span-zero

  -- The elapsed time since `b`, to `a`: the negation of
  -- `zoned-until a b`.
  zoned-since :: Zoned -> Zoned -> Span Clock
  zoned-since a b = span-negate (zoned-until a b)

  -- The span from `a` to `b` under the options (the header's
  -- DIFFERENCE law): 2024-05-31T00:00 until 2024-09-30T00:00 in New
  -- York with largest `Month` is 3 months 30 days.
  --   Err ZonedBadUnits      largest below smallest
  --   Err ZonedBadIncrement  the increment is not valid for the
  --                          smallest unit
  --   Err ZonedZonesDiffer   largest is `Day` or larger and the zones'
  --                          names differ
  --   Err ZonedOutOfRange    an intermediate reading or a rounding
  --                          candidate leaves the range
  --   Err ZonedUnitLimit     a unit of the result passes its limit
  zoned-until-with :: ZonedDifference -> Zoned -> Zoned -> Result ZonedError (Span Calendar)
  zoned-until-with o a b = difference False o a b

  zoned-since-with :: ZonedDifference -> Zoned -> Zoned -> Result ZonedError (Span Calendar)
  zoned-since-with o a b = negated (difference True o a b)

  -- -----------------------------------------------------------------
  -- Re-resolving a civil reading for a value that has an offset.
  -- -----------------------------------------------------------------

  -- InterpretISODateTimeOffset with "prefer": the offset `o` when the
  -- reading can have it, otherwise the strategy.
  prefer :: Disambiguation -> TimeZone -> Offset -> DateTime -> Result ZonedError Timestamp
  prefer s z o d =
    let a = zone-to-ambiguous z d in
    let keep = case a of
                 Unambiguous _ -> False
                 Gap _ _ -> False
                 Fold before after -> o == before || o == after in
    if keep then (case offset-datetime-to-timestamp o d of
                    None -> Err ZonedOutOfRange
                    Some t -> Ok t)
    else case ambiguous-resolve s d a of
      Err e -> Err (of-ambiguity e)
      Ok t -> Ok t

  -- The value with its civil date, time of day or both replaced
  -- (ZonedDateTime.prototype.with), keeping the value's offset when
  -- the new reading can have it and otherwise resolving by the
  -- strategy.
  --   Err ZonedRejectedGap, ZonedRejectedFold  the strategy is
  --                        `Reject` and the offset could not be kept
  --   Err ZonedOutOfRange  the instant leaves the range
  zoned-with-datetime-resolving :: Disambiguation -> DateTime -> Zoned -> Result ZonedError Zoned
  zoned-with-datetime-resolving s d x =
    let z = zoned-time-zone x in
    in-zone z (prefer s z (zoned-offset x) d)

  zoned-with-date-resolving :: Disambiguation -> Date -> Zoned -> Result ZonedError Zoned
  zoned-with-date-resolving s d x = zoned-with-datetime-resolving s (DT.datetime d (zoned-time-of-day x)) x

  zoned-with-time-resolving :: Disambiguation -> TimeOfDay -> Zoned -> Result ZonedError Zoned
  zoned-with-time-resolving s t x = zoned-with-datetime-resolving s (DT.datetime (zoned-date x) t) x

  -- The same, `Compatible`.
  zoned-with-datetime :: DateTime -> Zoned -> Result ZonedError Zoned
  zoned-with-datetime d x = zoned-with-datetime-resolving Compatible d x

  zoned-with-date :: Date -> Zoned -> Result ZonedError Zoned
  zoned-with-date d x = zoned-with-date-resolving Compatible d x

  zoned-with-time :: TimeOfDay -> Zoned -> Result ZonedError Zoned
  zoned-with-time t x = zoned-with-time-resolving Compatible t x

  -- -----------------------------------------------------------------
  -- Rounding a value.
  -- -----------------------------------------------------------------

  -- The options of `zoned-round-with` (T7): smallest unit, increment,
  -- mode.
  data ZonedRound where
    MkZonedRound :: TimeUnit -> I64 -> RoundMode -> ZonedRound

  -- `Nanosecond`, 1, `HalfExpand`: rounds nothing until a unit is set.
  zoned-round-default :: ZonedRound
  zoned-round-default = MkZonedRound Nanosecond 1 HalfExpand

  -- The common case: the default with a smallest unit.
  zoned-round-to :: TimeUnit -> ZonedRound
  zoned-round-to u = MkZonedRound u 1 HalfExpand

  implicit instance RoundingOptions_ZonedRound :: RoundingOptions ZonedRound where
    with-smallest u o = case o of
      MkZonedRound _ i m -> MkZonedRound u i m
    with-increment n o = case o of
      MkZonedRound s _ m -> MkZonedRound s n m
    with-mode md o = case o of
      MkZonedRound s i _ -> MkZonedRound s i md

  -- Rounding to the day: the elapsed time since the start of the civil
  -- day is rounded to a multiple of that day's real length, so the
  -- result is the start of this day or of the next. (The time is
  -- within one day and the quantum is the day, so the kernel cannot
  -- refuse.)
  round-day :: RoundMode -> Zoned -> Result ZonedError Zoned
  round-day mode x =
    let z = zoned-time-zone x in
    case start-of-date z (zoned-date x) of
      None -> Err ZonedOutOfRange
      Some a -> case zoned-start-of-next-day x of
        None -> Err ZonedOutOfRange
        Some nx ->
          let sn = nanos a in
          let len = sub128 (nanos (zoned-timestamp nx)) sn in
          case round-i128 mode len (sub128 (nanos (zoned-timestamp x)) sn) of
            None -> Err ZonedOutOfRange
            Some r -> in-zone z (instant-plus a r)

  -- Round a value (the header's ROUND law): 17:46:20.123456789 on
  -- 2024-11-02 in New York to 30 minutes is 18:00 -04:00; 11:45:20 on
  -- 2024-11-03 to `Day` is 2024-11-04T00:00 -05:00, because 11:45 is
  -- past the middle of a 25-hour day.
  --   Err ZonedBadUnits      week or larger
  --   Err ZonedBadIncrement  the increment does not divide the next
  --                          larger unit, or equals it; for `Day`,
  --                          anything but 1
  --   Err ZonedOutOfRange    the result leaves the range
  zoned-round-with :: ZonedRound -> Zoned -> Result ZonedError Zoned
  zoned-round-with o x = case o of
    MkZonedRound unit inc mode ->
      if unit-rank unit > 6 then Err ZonedBadUnits
      else if not (unit-round-increment-ok unit inc) then Err ZonedBadIncrement
      else if unit-eq unit Nanosecond && inc == 1 then Ok x
      else if unit-eq unit Day then round-day mode x
      else case datetime-round (with-mode mode (with-increment inc (round-to unit))) (zoned-datetime x) of
        Err e -> Err (of-span-error e)
        Ok d -> zoned-with-datetime d x

  -- Round to a unit, increment 1, `HalfExpand`.
  zoned-round :: TimeUnit -> Zoned -> Result ZonedError Zoned
  zoned-round u x = zoned-round-with (zoned-round-to u) x

  -- -----------------------------------------------------------------
  -- Spans relative to a zoned anchor.
  -- -----------------------------------------------------------------

  -- Where the anchor plus the span lands (`Compatible`).
  goal :: forall (c :: SpanClass). Zoned -> Span c -> Result ZonedError Timestamp
  goal x s = add-core Compatible (zoned-time-zone x) (zoned-timestamp x) (parts-of s)

  -- Round any span, measured from the anchor (Temporal's
  -- Duration.prototype.round with a zoned relativeTo), with the same
  -- options value as `span-round`.
  --   Err ZonedBadUnits, ZonedBadIncrement  as `span-round`
  --   Err ZonedOutOfRange  the anchor plus the span, an intermediate
  --                        reading or a rounding candidate leaves the
  --                        range
  --   Err ZonedUnitLimit   a unit of the result passes its limit
  span-round-zoned :: forall (c :: SpanClass). SpanRound -> Zoned -> Span c -> Result ZonedError (Span Calendar)
  span-round-zoned o x s = case span-round-largest o s of
    Err e -> Err (of-span-error e)
    Ok largest -> case goal x s of
      Err e -> Err e
      Ok g -> diff-rounded (zoned-time-zone x) (zoned-timestamp x) g largest (span-round-smallest o) (span-round-increment o) (span-round-mode o)

  -- Respell any span with units up to `largest`, measured from the
  -- anchor (T8: not rounding). 2756 hours from 2020-01-01T00:00 in
  -- Europe/Amsterdam to `Year` is 3 months 23 days 21 hours; 1 day
  -- from 2024-11-03T00:00 in New York to `Hour` is 25 hours.
  span-balance-zoned :: forall (c :: SpanClass). TimeUnit -> Zoned -> Span c -> Result ZonedError (Span Calendar)
  span-balance-zoned largest x s = case goal x s of
    Err e -> Err e
    Ok g -> diff-rounded (zoned-time-zone x) (zoned-timestamp x) g largest Nanosecond 1 Trunc

  gcd128 :: I128 -> I128 -> I128
  gcd128 a b = if eq-i128 b 0 then a else case checked-rem-i128 a b of
    None -> a
    Some r -> gcd128 b r

  -- A fraction in lowest terms; `d` is positive.
  reduce :: I128 -> I128 -> (I128, I128)
  reduce n d =
    let g = gcd128 (abs128 n) d in
    if lt-i128 g 2 then (n, d)
    else case checked-div-i128 n g of
      None -> (n, d)
      Some qn -> case checked-div-i128 d g of
        None -> (n, d)
        Some qd -> (qn, qd)

  -- The exact length of any span in `unit`, measured from the anchor,
  -- as a reduced fraction with a positive denominator (Temporal's
  -- Duration.prototype.total with a zoned relativeTo). `Hour` and
  -- smaller: the elapsed time over the unit's length. `Day` and
  -- larger: the whole units plus the fraction of the next one, the
  -- next one measured in the zone (1 hour from 2024-11-03T00:00 in
  -- New York is 1/25 of a day).
  --   Err ZonedOutOfRange  the anchor plus the span, or the end of
  --                        the unit being measured, leaves the range
  span-total-zoned :: forall (c :: SpanClass). TimeUnit -> Zoned -> Span c -> Result ZonedError (I128, I128)
  span-total-zoned unit x s = case goal x s of
    Err e -> Err e
    Ok g ->
      let z = zoned-time-zone x in
      let a = zoned-timestamp x in
      case unit-nanos unit of
        Some len -> Ok (reduce (sub128 (nanos g) (nanos a)) len)
        None ->
          if a == g then Ok (0, 1)
          else case diff-zoned z a g unit of
            Err e -> Err e
            Ok p ->
              let sign = direction p in
              case settled z (nanos a) (nanos g) (zoned-datetime x) sign p 1 unit of
                Err e -> Err e
                Ok pair -> case pair of
                  (win, _) -> case win of
                    MkWindow r1 _ _ sn en ->
                      let den = abs128 (sub128 en sn) in
                      let num = abs128 (sub128 (nanos g) sn) in
                      Ok (reduce (add128 (mul128 (wide r1) den) (mul128 (wide sign) num)) den)

  -- Any two spans by where they land from the anchor (Temporal's
  -- Duration.compare with a zoned relativeTo): 1 day and 24 hours are
  -- `EQ` from most instants and differ from 2024-11-03T00:00 in New
  -- York. Spans with no calendar units compare by elapsed time.
  --   Err ZonedOutOfRange  the anchor plus a span leaves the range
  span-compare-zoned :: forall (a :: SpanClass) (b :: SpanClass). Zoned -> Span a -> Span b -> Result ZonedError Ordering
  span-compare-zoned x a b =
    if span-fieldwise-eq a b then Ok EQ
    else if span-is-clock a && span-is-clock b then Ok (cmp128 (span-time-nanos a) (span-time-nanos b))
    else case goal x a of
      Err e -> Err e
      Ok ga -> case goal x b of
        Err e -> Err e
        Ok gb -> Ok (timestamp-cmp ga gb)

  cmp128 :: I128 -> I128 -> Ordering
  cmp128 a b = if lt-i128 a b then LT else if gt-i128 a b then GT else EQ

  -- The sum of any two spans measured from the anchor (Jiff's
  -- `Span::checked_add` with a zoned relative): the anchor moves by
  -- `a`, then by `b`, and the result is the difference from the anchor
  -- to that instant, spelled up to the larger of the two spans'
  -- largest units.
  --   Err ZonedOutOfRange  a step leaves the range
  --   Err ZonedUnitLimit   a unit of the result passes its limit
  span-checked-add-zoned :: forall (a :: SpanClass) (b :: SpanClass). Zoned -> Span a -> Span b -> Result ZonedError (Span Calendar)
  span-checked-add-zoned x a b =
    let z = zoned-time-zone x in
    let largest = unit-larger-of (span-largest-unit a) (span-largest-unit b) in
    case goal x a of
      Err e -> Err e
      Ok mid -> case add-core Compatible z mid (parts-of b) of
        Err e -> Err e
        Ok fin -> diff-rounded z (zoned-timestamp x) fin largest Nanosecond 1 Trunc

  span-checked-sub-zoned :: forall (a :: SpanClass) (b :: SpanClass). Zoned -> Span a -> Span b -> Result ZonedError (Span Calendar)
  span-checked-sub-zoned x a b = span-checked-add-zoned x a (span-negate b)
